@page "/learn/virtualize-scroll-to-item"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Open a list at any item · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Open a list at any item</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">A link points to message #300 of a long thread. How do you open the list right there?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Grids &amp; lists · Interactive Server · about 6 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> <code>InitialItemIndex</code> opens a <code>Virtualize</code> list at the item
        you choose. <code>ScrollToItemAsync</code> scrolls to any item from code. In .NET 10 the list always opened at
        the top, and you could only move it by scrolling to a pixel position.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a support thread of 420 messages, shown with <code>Virtualize</code>. Some are one-line log entries. Others
        are long emails. A link has asked for message #300.
    </p>
    <p>
        This is the .NET 10 way: guess each message's height, work out a pixel position, and scroll there with a bit
        of JavaScript. Message #300 is yellow.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="The thread, scrolled to an offset" Url="demo/scroll-to-item/incident/net10?message=300"
                Label=".NET 10: scroll to an offset" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            It has already missed. Only the yellow end of #300 shows at the top. Its header is above the box, out
            of sight. Type <code>35</code> into <b>Jump to message #</b> and press <b>Go</b>: it lands a few messages
            past #35.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>The guess is wrong</h2>
    <p>
        <code>Virtualize</code> only renders the messages near where you've scrolled. The rest are empty space, sized
        from an estimate of each message's height. <code>ItemSize</code> is your guess at that height. Here the page sets <code>ItemSize="120"</code>
        and scrolls to <i>(number − 1) × 120</i>. But real messages aren't 120px:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Where "Jump to #35" scrolls, and where #35 is</span>
        <pre class="snip">scrollTop = (35 − 1) × 120  = <span class="err">4,080px</span>   <span class="dim">every message 120px, the guess</span>
#35 really starts near      = <span class="ok">3,480px</span>   <span class="dim">the first 34 average about 100px</span>
                              <span class="err">600px too far: the box opens in the #40s</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        Near #300 it's worse. Once <code>Virtualize</code> has measured some messages, it sizes the empty space from
        their average, not your 120px. So the page's sum and the list disagree more the further down you go.
        And you can't correct it afterwards. The message you want isn't on the page yet, so there's nothing to
        scroll to.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Ask for the item, not a pixel</h2>
    <p>Tell <code>Virtualize</code> which item to open at. Call <code>ScrollToItemAsync</code> to go to one later.</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip">@@if (messages.Count &gt; 0)   <span class="dim">// InitialItemIndex applies once, so wait for the items</span>
{
    &lt;Virtualize @@ref="list" Items="messages" <span class="hl">InitialItemIndex="(Message ?? 1) - 1"</span>&gt;
        ...
    &lt;/Virtualize&gt;
}

<span class="dim">// Later, from a button, once the circuit has started (number is 1-based, like #35)</span>
<span class="hl">await list!.ScrollToItemAsync(number - 1);</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        <code>Message</code> is the <code>?message=300</code> from the link. <code>InitialItemIndex</code> and
        <code>ScrollToItemAsync</code> both take an index that starts at 0, so message #300 is index 299. <code>Virtualize</code> draws the messages around it and puts that message at the
        top of the box. It measures the real message, so heights don't matter. Here's the same thread and link.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same thread, with InitialItemIndex" Url="demo/scroll-to-item/incident?message=300"
                Label=".NET 11: InitialItemIndex">
        <Caption>
            #300 is at the top, yellow, from the start. Type <code>35</code> into <b>Jump to message #</b> and
            press <b>Go</b>: #35 is at the top.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>Here's the code for both lists, in the same order as the demos.</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/ScrollToItem/LinkedThreadNet10.razor" Feature="virtualize-scroll-to-item">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 1's list, the .NET 10 way. Highlighted: the workaround. It opens at the top, then scrolls to the
            guessed position. <code>scrollToOffset</code> is the page's own JavaScript. It waits until the list is tall
            enough to scroll, then sets <code>box.scrollTop = top</code>.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/ScrollToItem/LinkedThread.razor" Feature="virtualize-scroll-to-item">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 2's list, the .NET 11 way. Highlighted: the new API. The <code>@@if</code> on line 14 waits for the
            messages, because <code>InitialItemIndex</code> only applies once. The buttons on lines 8 and 9 stay
            disabled until the circuit (the live connection to the server) starts. Before then,
            <code>ScrollToItemAsync</code> throws.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Guess, or stop virtualizing</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 10, <code>Virtualize</code> couldn't start anywhere but the top, or scroll to an item. We tried each
        workaround on a thread like this one:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Three ways to open a .NET 10 list at a linked message, and what happened</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">scrollTop = (n - 1) * 120</pre>
                <span class="why">scroll the box to a calculated offset</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ lands a few messages late, as in Fig. 1: right only if every message is 120px</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">el.scrollIntoView()</pre>
                <span class="why">find the message by id and scroll it into view</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ finds nothing: only the first dozen messages are on the page</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">@@foreach (var m in messages)</pre>
                <span class="why">drop <code>Virtualize</code>, then scroll into view</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ lands exactly, but every message in the thread is on the page, so a long thread gets slow</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        The list was also empty until the circuit started, because the first HTML from the server had no messages
        in it. So the reader saw nothing, then the top of the thread, then a jump to roughly the right place.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>The list finds the item</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11, <code>InitialItemIndex</code> opens the list at an item, even in the first HTML from the server.
        <code>ScrollToItemAsync</code> goes to any item later. Both land exactly, whatever the heights, and the list
        stays virtualized. That's Fig. 2.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b><code>InitialItemIndex</code> applies once.</b> Changing it later does nothing. To open a different thread at its own place, give each thread's <code>Virtualize</code> a <code>@@key</code>, such as the thread's id. A new key makes Blazor build a fresh <code>Virtualize</code>, which applies <code>InitialItemIndex</code> again.</li>
        <li><b><code>ScrollToItemAsync</code> needs the circuit.</b> It throws <code>InvalidOperationException</code> until the circuit starts. Use <code>InitialItemIndex</code> for where the list opens.</li>
        <li><b>The last call wins.</b> A new <code>ScrollToItemAsync</code> cancels one still running. If the user scrolls, that wins over any call still running. Pass a <code>CancellationToken</code> to cancel one yourself.</li>
        <li><b>QuickGrid has the same pair,</b> <code>InitialItemIndex</code> and <code>ScrollToItemAsync</code>, for grids with <code>Virtualize="true"</code>.</li>
        <li><b>Early previews used other names.</b> Code written against Preview 6 says <code>InitialIndex</code> and <code>ScrollToIndexAsync</code>. Rename them.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
